I've tried to come up with an estimate for the probability of this to
happen.  The results are interesting for two reasons.  First, one might
wonder whether you should lock your cat out of the room, when playing
nethack :-), second there is the question whether the DevTeam should do
something to render this kind of accident less likely.  My own answers
to these questions have changed as my astonishment about various aspects
of the problem increased.

O.k., let's start with the basic assumptions.  There are about 100 keys
on the keyboard (mine has 105), and since I know nothing about special
patterns of preferences or patterns of motion of your cat, I will assume
that each key had the same probability to be hit by her (or by you
through her interaction).  Well, I will be more precise - the return key
has about three times the area of the others, so I will assign a basic
probability of 3/100 to it instead of the 1/100 for all the others.  The
enter key to the right of the number pad gets 2/100 for similar reasons.
This is an assumption of least-biased judgement which works well in
statistical mechanics.  It may work less well here, since we have far
fewer events but I don't see anything reasonable to replace it.

Next we need an estimate of how long a sequence of key signals was sent
to the computer during the event.  I will assume that your cat held
about 20 keys pressed at any moment and that she changed her position
every tenth of a second, which means she sent 200 characters per second.
According to your own estimate she did this for 2 seconds, so that would
be 400 characters.  We can assume them to have been sent sequentially,
for even if she kept several keys in the "down" position at any time,
the computer will have received them sequentially, separated by the
milliseconds difference between triggering.  And sequence matters. You
can try that out yourself - if you hit "q" and then "<alt>", keeping
both down, this is harmless, the game only wants to know what you would
like to quaff.  If you hit, on the other hand "<alt>" and then "q",
keeping both down, you will be asked whether you want to quit the game.

Since my calculation evolved during time, I will give it in the
historical sequence, moving from easy to - not difficult but a bit involved.

If you wanted to quit the game the way its designers intended you to,
you would hit the sequence "#q", then the game would present you with
the choice "quit" for the extended command you just started, to which
you can answer "<ret>" (return key). Then the game would ask you whether
you really want to quit, which you would answer by "y".  So you would
type the four-character sequence "#q<ret>y".  The probability for this
sequence to be hit randomly by your cat in one try would be
(1/100)^3*3/100 = 3/100,000,000, which is less than the chance to hit
the jackpot in the weekly German lottery (containing a prize of about a
million Euros).  Of course, your cat did not play the lottery only once,
she played it 400 times, which increases the total probability to about
1.2 in 100,000, which still is pretty low.  For example, you can compare
it with the probability to get killed accidentally by the mischievous
attack of your cat.  For that, three keys are sufficient.  Suppose your
wand of death was in slot "d", then the sequence "zd." would have made
Ellora commit suicide, with a flat probability of (1/100)^3=1/1,000,000,
which is 30 times higher than the probability of quitting.  The total
probability would still be low, at about 1/2,500, but that is at least
already in the range of a tenth of a percent.

So I was looking for possible modifiers of the above-mentioned
probability.  There are some that even reduce it.  If the sequence of
the four keys is preceded by "a","c","e","o" or "t" or any other key
after which the game expects a character telling it what you want to
apply, close, eat, etc., then the sequence "#q<ret>y" becomes harmless.
However, this modifier is negligible.  I don't think there are more
than ten keys acting this way, so we are talking about a reduction by a
factor of 0.9 or 90%, which does not matter - my estimates are
definitely less accurate than the 10% difference that would be produced
by this.  Similarly, we can neglect the fact that the sequences
"#qu<ret>y", "#qui<ret>y" and "#quit<ret>y" equally end the game as each
of these needs at least one letter more, and each letter reduces the
probability of the sequence by a factor of 100, so we are talking about
modifications in the percent range and below.

This was the state of my considerations, when I first mentioned this
topic in a discussion with Janis.  At that point I thought, the event is
so unlikely that one must consider your cat really having hit the
jackpot (but for bad luck rather than luck!), and it would be almost
paranoid to keep her out of the room to avoid such an unlikely event.
You would not see it happen a second time in your life.  But...

A first appreciable effect comes from the fact that the "<ret>" key can
be replaced by the "<enter>" key from the number pad.  Instead of the
probability 3/100 for the "<ret>" key alone, we have 3/100+2/100 for the
two keys that can alternatively confirm the "#q", which increases our
total probability to 2 in 100,000.  Much more important is another
effect, however.  If you type in anything *but* "y" or "n" after the
first three characters, then the game just waits until the right
character arrives.  This changes things drastically.  Now we don't deal
with one or two sequences of four characters but with all sequences
containing the sequence and any kind of garbage between the third
and the fourth.  Will only a "y" or an "n" end the input for the
command?  No.  The "<ret>" and "<enter>" keys are interpreted as
confirming the default, which is "n" (don't quit), moreover, if "<esc>"
is hit, the effect is the same.  Unfortunately, the "y" is not the only
key that acts confirming.  On the number pad (if "num lock" is off)
there is a key that corresponds to the direction key "y" (north-east)
and will just as well cause the game to quit, and there is another key
that sends a "y" to the computer on the small six-key pad between the
number pad and the alphanumeric pad.  Also, the "n" character is present
three times, since "n" is a direction as well (south-west).

Since it is difficult to sum up the probabilities for all the sequences
of different length that can end the game, I will make another
simplifying assumption.  Let us assume that the sequence after the first
three characters is long enough that one of the answers "yes" or "no" is
bound to appear.  Since the probability for "yes" is 3/100 and that for
"no" 9/100 (the combined area of the "<ret>", "<enter>", "<esc>" and
three "y" keys), it should be largely sufficient, if your cat had hit
the three first characters during the first second.  Then the 200
remaining inputs would have sent one of the two answers with probability
close to one, "yes" with a probability 1/4 and "no" with a probability 3/4.

That brings the total probability to a whopping 200*(1/100)^2*5/100*1/4
= 1 in 4,000, where the first factor of 200 describes the 200 attempts
of your cat in the first second to produce the "#q<ret>" or "#q<enter>"
at random.  This is almost as likely as the killer "zd.".  But we have
to modify the latter, too, since the "." unfortunately is also present
on the number pad, which moves the probability up to 1 in 1,250, and
this has to multiplied by a factor of 3, should your wand of death
happen to be in a slot describing a diagonal direction ("n","y","b", or
"u") and by a factor of 2 for horizontal or vertical directions
("h","j","k", or "l").  Moreover, that probability will of course
increase, if there are more than one wand of death in your inventory.
(It decreases, if the wand is in a slot for a capital letter, for which
you have to hit the <shift> key in addition, so it may be a good idea to
keep it in "D" rather than "d".)

Still the total probability of *not* being affected by one of these two
disasters is more than 99,5%.  I would not consider this as sufficient
sole reason to keep the cat away when I am playing (although I would
keep her away, if the thought of what can happen made me sufficiently
nervous, regardless of the low probabilities...).

Then I read in one of the posts following your story that you can quit
the game by typing "<alt>q" rather than "#q".  I had not been aware of
that - I knew that in the old times "<shift>q" was what you typed for
quitting, but this had to be changed to liberate the letter "Q" for the
command "quiver", and since I have so often seen the message "Use #quit
to quit the game", I somehow believed this is the only way now.  Well, I
thought that this would just increase the probability by another factor
of 2, which does not change things appreciably.  Nevertheless, careful
as I am :-), I tried it out, and I was shocked!  When you type "<alt>q",
the game does not wait for a return or enter key - it immediately asks
you for confirmation!  Now this is only a three-character sequence and,
much worse, if you send anything but the "yes" or "no" answer that the
game expects, it waits patiently until one of these answers arrives.
According to our elaborated scheme of calculation that will give us
200*(1/100)^2*1/4 = 1 in 200 (the factor 5/100 for the <ret> and <enter>
key just drops out), which is half a percent!  As can be seen easily,
quitting by this route accidentally is now *more* likely than zapping
yourself with a wand of death (which is not much of a relief, I suppose).

Now if you take into account that these are just rough estimates that
can easily be off by a factor of 5 or even 10 (due to inevitable
imponderabilities in the estimate), then the flat probability for this
to happen when your cat hops onto your keyboard might be as high as 5%!
Personally, I would draw the line here.  That is, my cat (if I had one)
would have to stay out! ;-)

But I also think that the DevTeam should simply remove the "shortcut"
to quitting that is provided by using the "<alt>" key instead of the "#"
one.  From my estimates it transpires that with 95% probability it was
the "<alt>q" command that ended your game rather than "#quit".  This
simple measure would save 19 out of 20 games that otherwise get lost by
accidents leading to a sequence of many random keys to be sent to the
computer.  Second, I think that the default behaviour should not be
triggered only if you type one of the equivalents of "n" but always if
you type anything but "y".  This would reduce the probability of
disaster by another factor of about 10.  Whether a paranoia patch,
requiring the full answer of "yes" to be typed to the question whether
one would like to quit, would then still be necessary, is a question of
personal taste.  Probabilities below a tenth of a percent don't perturb
me so much.  They affect less than one game in thousand and I don't even
know whether I will ever play that many :-).

